qs.append({"q":"Which of the following is identified as a major limitation (negative aspect) of the 19th-20th century socio-religious reform movements as a whole?","o":["Their narrow social base, being confined largely to the educated urban middle classes while ignoring the peasantry and urban poor","Their complete failure to address the position of women","Their total rejection of religious authority and tradition","Their exclusive focus on economic issues at the expense of social reform"],"a":0,"e":"A key limitation was the narrow social base of these movements — confined mainly to the educated, urban middle classes — leaving the vast peasantry and urban poor largely untouched."})

qs.append({"q":"According to the chapter's assessment, the tendency of Hindu reformers to confine their praise to ancient Indian history, while some Muslim reformers looked to medieval or West Asian history for pride, contributed to:","o":["A notion of two separate peoples and increased communal consciousness","Complete religious unity between Hindus and Muslims","The rapid decline of all religious identities","Universal acceptance of a single composite Indian culture"],"a":0,"e":"This selective historical emphasis by different communities is cited as a factor that reinforced a sense of two separate peoples, contributing to the growth of communal consciousness alongside national consciousness."})

